// BMET Academy question bank, units 6 to 8. All questions are original.
// Fields: id, t (lesson id), q (stem), a (four options), c (index of correct option), e (explanation).
window.FRA = window.FRA || {};
FRA.questions = FRA.questions || [];
FRA.questions.push(
// u6l1
{id:"u6l1-1",t:"u6l1",q:"A clinic sends you an otoscope because 'the light is too dim to see anything.' What does the device do, and where do you look first?",a:["It measures ear temperature, so check the infrared sensor","It lights and magnifies the ear canal, so check the lamp, the battery or charger contacts, and the specula","It lights the back of the eye, so check the lens dial","It measures pressure in the ear, so check for leaks"],c:1,e:"An otoscope lights and magnifies the ear canal and eardrum through a disposable or cleanable specula on a battery or rechargeable handle. A dim lamp is almost always the lamp itself, the battery, or dirty charger contacts, not the optics."},
{id:"u6l1-2",t:"u6l1",q:"You are inspecting a floor scale in an outpatient clinic. Which check sequence is correct, and what equipment does it need?",a:["Weigh one staff member and compare with their known weight","Load the maximum weight only, since that is the worst case","Zero the scale empty, then compare certified calibration weights at a low, middle, and high load","Compare it against another scale on the same floor"],c:2,e:"Zeroing corrects the offset, so a scale that was not zeroed is wrong by the same amount at every load. Certified weights at three loads then test the span across the range, and those weights carry their own calibration due date."},
{id:"u6l1-3",t:"u6l1",q:"A laboratory centrifuge shakes hard and trips its imbalance warning as soon as it spins up. What is the first thing to check, and why is the lid interlock important?",a:["The timer, because a wrong time causes vibration","The motor brushes, because they cause the shake","The power cord, because low voltage causes imbalance","The tube load for balanced pairs opposite each other; the interlock keeps the lid shut until the rotor stops"],c:3,e:"Tubes must be loaded in balanced pairs opposite each other, because an unbalanced rotor at thousands of revolutions per minute can destroy itself. The lid interlock must hold the lid closed while the rotor turns and refuse to release until it stops."},
{id:"u6l1-4",t:"u6l1",q:"A nurse reports that a bladder scanner gives wildly varying volumes on the same patient. Which explanation fits the way the device measures?",a:["Ultrasound cannot cross an air gap, so missing gel or a bad aim off the midline gives nonsense volumes","The battery is low, which always halves the reading","The probe needs a probe cover like a thermometer","It is measuring weight, so the patient must stand still"],c:0,e:"A bladder scanner is handheld ultrasound that computes the urine volume left in the bladder. Sound will not cross an air gap, so coupling gel is required, and the probe must be aimed over the midline above the pubic bone."},
{id:"u6l1-5",t:"u6l1",q:"Which instrument checks whether a centrifuge really spins at the speed its dial claims?",a:["A digital multimeter on the motor leads","A tachometer reading revolutions per minute","A stopwatch and a graduated cylinder","A patient simulator"],c:1,e:"A tachometer measures rotational speed in revolutions per minute, optically off a reflective mark or by contact, and it is the instrument that verifies a centrifuge's dial against reality. Like every instrument, it needs a current calibration sticker."},

// u6l2
{id:"u6l2-1",t:"u6l2",q:"A monitor on a telemetry floor keeps raising an asystole alarm on a patient who is sitting up and talking. What does the monitor count, and what is the likely cause?",a:["It counts breaths, so the cause is a disconnected respiration cable","It counts cuff cycles, so the NIBP hose is leaking","It counts the QRS complex, so a dried-out electrode or a broken lead wire is the likely cause","It counts light pulses, so the oximeter probe has failed"],c:2,e:"A monitor derives heart rate by counting QRS complexes from the ECG signal, so anything that spoils that signal mimics a flat line. Dried-out electrode gel and lead wires broken from flexing at the ends are the two usual causes."},
{id:"u6l2-2",t:"u6l2",q:"A patient's blood pressure reads much higher on a bedside monitor than on the manual check a nurse just performed. Which measurement error fits first?",a:["The cycle interval is too short, which reads high","The cuff is too large for the arm, which reads high","The hose is too long, which reads high","The cuff is too small for the arm, which reads high"],c:3,e:"Non-invasive blood pressure is oscillometric, and cuff size is the biggest error source: a cuff too small reads high and a cuff too large reads low. The bladder should wrap roughly 80% of the arm's circumference."},
{id:"u6l2-3",t:"u6l2",q:"Which two wavelengths of light does a pulse oximeter use, and what defeats the measurement?",a:["Red at about 650 to 660 nm and infrared at about 805 to 940 nm; motion, poor perfusion, or nail polish defeat it","Ultraviolet and infrared; bright room lighting always defeats it","Green and blue; a warm finger defeats it","Infrared only; a cold room defeats it"],c:0,e:"Pulse oximetry shines red light at about 650 to 660 nm and infrared at about 805 to 940 nm through tissue and compares absorption. It needs a pulse to work from, so motion, cold or poorly perfused fingers, and dark nail polish all defeat it."},
{id:"u6l2-4",t:"u6l2",q:"A capnograph on a sidestream sensor shows a falling then flat carbon dioxide waveform while the patient is clearly breathing. What is the normal value, and what should you suspect?",a:["95 to 100%; a cracked optical sensor","35 to 45 mmHg; water or secretions blocking the sample line or a full water trap","12 to 20 breaths per minute; a loose ECG electrode","120/80 mmHg; a kinked cuff hose"],c:1,e:"End-tidal carbon dioxide is normally 35 to 45 mmHg. A sidestream sensor draws a gas sample through thin tubing, so condensation, secretions, or a neglected water trap block the sample path and flatten the waveform."},
{id:"u6l2-5",t:"u6l2",q:"Telemetry patients on one wing drop off the central station for a few seconds at a time, several times a shift. Which explanation matches how telemetry works?",a:["The central station needs a new printer driver","Every dropout is a real rhythm change and must be charted","A transmitter battery or a coverage hole in the antenna system, since telemetry sends vitals by radio on WMTS bands","The electrodes must be replaced with gel-free pads"],c:2,e:"Telemetry sends the same measurements by radio from a battery-powered transmitter through ceiling antennas to a central station, on WMTS bands reserved for medical telemetry. Dropouts point at batteries or antenna coverage, not at the patient."},

// u6l3
{id:"u6l3-1",t:"u6l3",q:"You connect a patient simulator to a monitor and every number matches its tolerance. A colleague says the monitor is now cleared for patient use. What is missing?",a:["Nothing; a simulator check covers performance and safety","A software update from the manufacturer","A second simulator at a different rate","The electrical safety test, because a simulator draws no current and proves nothing about ground or leakage"],c:3,e:"A patient simulator supplies known signals and draws essentially no current, so it verifies performance only. Ground resistance and leakage currents are measured by an electrical safety analyzer, and that test comes before the performance work."},
{id:"u6l3-2",t:"u6l3",q:"A simulator is set to 60 beats per minute and the monitor displays 63. The service manual specifies plus or minus 2 beats per minute. What is the verdict?",a:["Fail, because the error of 3 beats per minute exceeds the manual's tolerance","Pass, because the monitor is within 5%","Pass, because 3 beats is clinically insignificant","Cannot be judged without a second simulator"],c:0,e:"Performance verification compares the reading against the published tolerance, not against what looks reasonable. The error is the reading minus the known input, so 63 minus 60 is 3 beats per minute, outside a plus or minus 2 limit, and the monitor fails."},
{id:"u6l3-3",t:"u6l3",q:"Which simulator setting is used to prove that a monitor alarms when the heart stops producing a signal?",a:["Normal sinus rhythm at 40 beats per minute","Asystole, which must make the monitor read zero and raise an alarm","A 97% saturation setting on the finger simulator","A 120/80 pressure setting"],c:1,e:"Asystole is a flat line, so the monitor must display a rate of zero and alarm. Alarm checks with asystole and ventricular fibrillation are part of every monitor inspection, because a monitor that measures well and never alarms is dangerous."},
{id:"u6l3-4",t:"u6l3",q:"Which output does a patient simulator provide for the respiration channel of a bedside monitor?",a:["A small flow of air through the ECG cable","A carbon dioxide sample at 40 mmHg","A changing impedance across the ECG leads, as a breathing chest produces","A pressure pulse through the cuff hose"],c:2,e:"Monitors measure respiration by watching impedance change across the ECG electrodes as the chest moves, so the simulator varies that impedance. The same simulator also produces ECG rhythms, SpO2 through a finger simulator, NIBP pressures, and temperature resistances."},
{id:"u6l3-5",t:"u6l3",q:"In a monitor inspection, where do the cable and lead wire checks belong and why?",a:["Last, after the simulator, since cables rarely fail","Only when the clinical staff complain about them","They are not part of an inspection at all","Early, right after the visual, because lead wires fail at the ends and a broken one makes every later reading lie"],c:3,e:"Lead wires and trunk cables are the most common fault in the whole device, failing at the ends from flexing. Checking them early, with continuity, prevents hours spent chasing a measurement error that was really an accessory."},

// u6l4
{id:"u6l4-1",t:"u6l4",q:"A nurse programs a large-volume pump with a rate of 125 and a VTBI of 500. What has she told the pump?",a:["Deliver 125 mL/h until a total of 500 mL has been infused, then stop or drop to a keep vein open trickle","Deliver 125 mL in total and stop after 500 minutes","Deliver 125 drops per minute for 500 minutes","Deliver 500 mL/h for 125 minutes"],c:0,e:"Rate is programmed in mL/h and says how fast; volume to be infused is the total to deliver before the pump stops and alarms. After that total, the pump either stops or drops to the keep vein open rate so the line does not clot."},
{id:"u6l4-2",t:"u6l4",q:"An infusion pump alarms repeatedly for air in line on a freshly started infusion. What does that alarm detect, and what is the usual cause?",a:["A pressure sensor sees a closed clamp downstream","An ultrasonic detector sees bubbles in the tubing, usually because the set was purged carelessly at setup","The drug library rejected the entered dose","The battery is below its runtime specification"],c:1,e:"The air-in-line detector is ultrasonic and sits around the tubing. Bubbles left by an incompletely primed set are the common cause, which is why purging the set properly at setup prevents most of these calls."},
{id:"u6l4-3",t:"u6l4",q:"Why are enteral feeding sets made with ENFit connectors that cannot join an intravenous line?",a:["To make them cheaper to manufacture","To stop the pump from over-delivering","Because incompatibility is the safety feature: formula delivered into a vein is fatal","Because feeding pumps use higher pressure"],c:2,e:"Feeding pumps deliver formula into the stomach or intestine. Rather than relying on every user remembering never to connect a feeding set to a vein, the connector was deliberately designed so the wrong connection cannot physically be made."},
{id:"u6l4-4",t:"u6l4",q:"A syringe pump delivers noticeably less than it should at a low rate, and the mechanism looks sound. What setting should you check first?",a:["The keep vein open rate","The air-in-line sensitivity","The drug library version","The syringe brand and size selected on the pump"],c:3,e:"A syringe pump computes flow from the plunger's travel and the barrel's diameter, so it must be told which syringe is loaded. The wrong syringe profile produces a confidently wrong flow with no alarm at all."},
{id:"u6l4-5",t:"u6l4",q:"A technician removes a loaded administration set from a running pump and no fluid runs freely from the bag. Which feature caused that?",a:["Free-flow protection: the set's own clamp closes when the set leaves the pump","The occlusion alarm threshold","The keep vein open setting","The drug library's hard limit"],c:0,e:"Free-flow protection is a clamp built into the administration set that closes by itself when the set is removed from the pumping channel, so gravity cannot empty the bag into the patient at an uncontrolled rate."},

// u6l5
{id:"u6l5-1",t:"u6l5",q:"You collect 62 mL in a 30-minute timed collection from a pump set to 120 mL/h. What rate did the pump actually deliver?",a:["62 mL/h","124 mL/h","31 mL/h","186 mL/h"],c:1,e:"Convert the collection back to the pump's units with rate = volume / minutes x 60, so 62 / 30 x 60 = 124 mL/h. A 30-minute collection is simply doubled to give the hourly rate."},
{id:"u6l5-2",t:"u6l5",q:"A pump set to 120 mL/h delivered a measured 124 mL/h. What is the percent error, and does it pass a plus or minus 5% specification?",a:["-3.3%, which passes","+4.0%, which fails","+3.3%, which passes","+33%, which fails"],c:2,e:"Percent error is (delivered minus set) / set x 100, so (124 minus 120) / 120 x 100 = +3.3%. The size of the error is inside the 5% band, so the check passes, and the positive sign says the pump over-delivers."},
{id:"u6l5-3",t:"u6l5",q:"Why does the flow check procedure call for a collection of at least 30 minutes instead of 5?",a:["The pump needs half an hour to warm up","A cylinder only reads correctly when nearly full","Shorter collections are forbidden by the privacy rules","A longer collection averages the peristaltic pulses and makes a 1 mL reading error small"],c:3,e:"A peristaltic mechanism delivers in small pushes, so a short collection can land on a favorable part of the cycle. Length also shrinks your own reading error: 1 mL misread on a 10 mL collection is 10%, while on 60 mL it is under 2%."},
{id:"u6l5-4",t:"u6l5",q:"Your shop has a good balance but every graduated cylinder is signed out. How can you still run a flow check, and what conversion makes it work?",a:["Weigh the collected water, since 1 g of water equals 1 mL","Weigh the pump before and after the run","Count drops in the drip chamber and use the drop factor","Use the pump's own volume infused display as the measurement"],c:0,e:"The gravimetric method tares a container on a balance, collects for the timed period, and weighs again. Water is convenient because 1 g of water equals 1 mL, and weighing removes meniscus and parallax reading error entirely."},
{id:"u6l5-5",t:"u6l5",q:"Besides flow accuracy, which group of tests belongs in a complete infusion pump inspection?",a:["Only a visual inspection and a cleaning","Occlusion alarm, air-in-line alarm, battery run time, and the free-flow clamp","Delivered energy, charge time, and sync delay","Ground resistance only, since flow covers performance"],c:1,e:"A full inspection provokes each protective feature: clamp the line for the occlusion alarm, introduce air for the detector, time the battery against its specification, and confirm the set's free-flow clamp closes when the door opens."},

// u6l6
{id:"u6l6-1",t:"u6l6",q:"A crash cart defibrillator is being inspected. What are the adult energy figures for biphasic and monophasic waveforms, and the pediatric dose?",a:["Biphasic 360 J, monophasic 120 to 200 J, pediatric 10 J/kg","Biphasic 50 J, monophasic 100 J, pediatric 1 J/kg","Biphasic 120 to 200 J, monophasic 360 J, pediatric 2 to 4 J/kg","Biphasic and monophasic are both 200 J, pediatric 5 J/kg"],c:2,e:"A biphasic waveform reverses current partway through, so adult energies are 120 to 200 J by the manufacturer's protocol, while an older monophasic unit needs 360 J. Pediatric dosing is weight based at 2 to 4 J/kg."},
{id:"u6l6-2",t:"u6l6",q:"What load does a defibrillator analyzer present, and what does that load represent?",a:["An open circuit, so the capacitor discharges into the air","A 500 ohm load, standing in for dry skin","A 1 ohm load, so all the energy is absorbed","A 50 ohm load, standing in for the impedance of a patient's chest"],c:3,e:"The analyzer presents a 50 ohm load, the standard stand-in for a chest, and measures the energy that actually arrives there. That is why the analyzer reports delivered energy rather than what the capacitor stored."},
{id:"u6l6-3",t:"u6l6",q:"A clinician asks why cardioversion of a fast but organized rhythm is synchronized while defibrillation is not. What is the answer?",a:["Synchronized mode times the shock to the QRS complex; a chaotic rhythm has no complex to synchronize to","Synchronization saves battery charge","Synchronization halves the delivered energy","Defibrillation is synchronized to the T wave instead"],c:0,e:"Synchronized cardioversion waits for the QRS complex so the shock does not land in the vulnerable part of the cycle. Ventricular fibrillation has no organized complex, so defibrillation is unsynchronized and fires on the operator's command."},
{id:"u6l6-4",t:"u6l6",q:"An AED on a wall bracket is chirping. What is it telling you, and what are the two parts to check first?",a:["It is out of paper; load paper and clear the alarm","It has failed its self-test; check the battery and the pad expiry dates","It needs a network connection; check the wireless settings","It needs to be discharged into the air to reset it"],c:1,e:"An AED runs periodic self-tests and chirps when one fails. Battery condition and expired or dried-out pads are the two most common causes, and expired pads are the most common reason a defibrillator fails when it is needed."},
{id:"u6l6-5",t:"u6l6",q:"You need to open a defibrillator's case to replace a cooling fan. Which hazard governs the job, and what do you do about it?",a:["The battery, so just unplug the mains cord","The display, so discharge static before touching the screen","The capacitor holds a lethal charge after power off, so dump the charge into the analyzer or use the internal dump and confirm it","Nothing special; with power off the unit is safe"],c:2,e:"The stored energy is in a capacitor that does not care whether the power is off or the battery is out. Discharge into the analyzer or use the unit's internal dump and verify it before opening the case, and never discharge into the air or onto a bench."},

// u6l7
{id:"u6l7-1",t:"u6l7",q:"A wall suction regulator will not pull anywhere near its set level. In what order should you look for the leak?",a:["The battery, then the charger, then the power cord","The motor, then the brushes, then the bearings","The gauge face, then the knob, then the wall outlet color","Lid seal, canister and float, tubing, then the filter"],c:3,e:"Clamp the tubing at the patient end and watch the gauge reach and hold the set level. If it will not reach, the air is getting in somewhere, so work the path: lid seal, canister and float, tubing, then the filter, which is the usual culprit."},
{id:"u6l7-2",t:"u6l7",q:"What does a sequential compression device deliver to a patient, and what does its inspection measure?",a:["A timed squeeze on the legs through sleeves; pressure accuracy, cycle timing, and sleeve and tubing leaks","Negative pressure through a canister; the vacuum level","Warm air through a blanket; the temperature at setpoint","Small electrical pulses through electrodes; output amplitude and rate"],c:0,e:"A sequential compression device inflates sleeves on the calves or thighs in a timed cycle to keep blood moving and prevent clots. The inspection checks pressure against a reference gauge, the cycle timing, and leaks in the sleeves and tubing."},
{id:"u6l7-3",t:"u6l7",q:"Why does every patient warming device get two separate temperature tests rather than one?",a:["One tests the heater and one tests the fan","The controller holding the setpoint and the independent over-temperature cutoff are different things and can fail separately","Regulations require two readings of the same value","One is in Celsius and one is in Fahrenheit"],c:1,e:"A device can hold its set temperature perfectly while its protective cutoff is dead, and that device burns somebody the day the controller fails. The accuracy check and the over-temperature cutoff test are therefore run as two separate tests."},
{id:"u6l7-4",t:"u6l7",q:"A physical therapy department reports no output from a TENS unit. What does the device deliver, and where does the fault usually sit?",a:["A timed squeeze; the sleeve","Deep heating by sound energy; the transducer head","Small electrical pulses through skin electrodes; the electrodes, lead wires, or battery","Warm water; the hose fittings"],c:2,e:"Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation sends small adjustable pulses through skin electrodes to block pain. As with most therapy equipment, the fault is nearly always the electrodes, the lead wires, or the battery rather than the generator."},
{id:"u6l7-5",t:"u6l7",q:"Which hazard makes the timer on a therapeutic ultrasound unit a safety-relevant item to verify?",a:["Electrical shock from the handpiece cable","A clot, because the device compresses tissue","Radiation exposure to the operator","A burn, because a stalled head left on in one place keeps depositing energy in the tissue"],c:3,e:"Therapeutic ultrasound warms tissue below the skin through a handheld head and coupling gel. If the head stops moving or the timer fails to end the treatment, energy keeps going into one spot, so output power and the timer are both verified."},

// u6l8
{id:"u6l8-1",t:"u6l8",q:"A cover screw on a monitor has a printed torque value of 8 inch-pounds. Why does the number matter in both directions?",a:["Too little lets the fastener back out or a gasket weep; too much strips threads, cracks plastic, or crushes a seal","Only over-tightening matters; a loose screw is harmless","Only under-tightening matters; plastic cannot be cracked by a screwdriver","The number is advisory and any snug fit is acceptable"],c:0,e:"Torque is twisting force in inch-pounds or newton-meters, and the printed value is what the joint was designed for. Under-torque loses contact and lets fasteners walk out under vibration; over-torque strips threads, cracks bosses, and crushes seals."},
{id:"u6l8-2",t:"u6l8",q:"A technician uses a torque wrench to break loose a seized fastener. What has that done, and why does it matter later?",a:["Nothing, as long as the fastener came free","It can knock the wrench out of calibration, so every value it sets afterward is unreliable","It improves the wrench's range","It only damages the fastener"],c:1,e:"A torque wrench is a calibrated instrument. Using it as a breaker bar overloads the mechanism, and once it reads wrong every joint you set with it is wrong too, with no indication on the record that anything happened."},
{id:"u6l8-3",t:"u6l8",q:"Before measuring chassis leakage on a device, you notice the analyzer's calibration sticker expired last month. What should you do?",a:["Run the test and note the expired sticker in the record","Run the test, since leakage readings do not depend on calibration","Swap to an instrument with current calibration before running the test","Run the test twice and average the readings"],c:2,e:"A reading taken with an out-of-calibration instrument proves nothing, because nobody can say which way it was wrong. Check the sticker before you check the device, and swap instruments rather than documenting an invalid result."},
{id:"u6l8-4",t:"u6l8",q:"You need to open an enclosure held by six-point star screws with a pin in the center of each. Which driver is correct?",a:["A flat blade, turned carefully","A Phillips driver one size up","Needle-nose pliers on the screw head","A security Torx bit with a hole for the center pin"],c:3,e:"Torx is the six-point star, and the version with a pin in the middle needs a security bit bored to clear that pin. Manufacturers use security heads to keep users out of enclosures, and forcing the wrong driver destroys the head."},
{id:"u6l8-5",t:"u6l8",q:"A solder joint you just made is a dull gray ball sitting on the pad. What is wrong with it, and what does it mean for the device?",a:["It is a cold joint that will conduct now and fail intermittently later","It is a good joint; dull means the flux burned off","It is over-heated and should be left alone","It is normal for lead-free solder and needs no attention"],c:0,e:"A sound joint is shiny and concave with the wire's outline visible under it. A dull ball means the solder was melted onto a joint that never got hot enough to wet, so it conducts today and becomes an intermittent fault months later."},

// u6l9
{id:"u6l9-1",t:"u6l9",q:"A crate holding a new vital signs monitor has just been opened on your bench. What is the first step of the incoming inspection?",a:["Plug it in and run the electrical safety test","Check the shipment against the packing slip and the purchase order, then inspect for damage and completeness","Create the CMMS record and assign a PM interval","Carry it to the floor and train the users"],c:1,e:"Paperwork and eyes come first, because they are the only steps that can still be undone cheaply: a wrong model goes back, shipping damage is the carrier's problem, and powering a damaged device can turn a claim into a repair you own."},
{id:"u6l9-2",t:"u6l9",q:"Why are the electrical safety readings from an incoming inspection written down as numbers rather than as the word 'pass'?",a:["Because the record needs to be long","Because the manufacturer requires the format","Because they are the baseline every future test is compared against, which is how trends become visible","Because the limits change every year"],c:2,e:"A device that read 0.08 ohm and 40 microamps when new and reads 0.3 ohm and 320 microamps three years later is still passing and is also telling you a cord and insulation are failing. Only recorded numbers make that trend visible."},
{id:"u6l9-3",t:"u6l9",q:"In an incoming inspection, where does configuration of alarm limits, the clock, and the network settings belong?",a:["Before the physical inspection, so the device is ready","It is the clinical staff's job, not the technician's","Only after the device has been in service a month","After the function test has proved the device works, and before release"],c:3,e:"Configuration comes after the function test because you set up what you have proved works, and before release because the floor should receive the device configured the way the department agreed. Training then happens before the device goes into use."},
{id:"u6l9-4",t:"u6l9",q:"Which list is the correct order for a scheduled maintenance procedure?",a:["Interval and checklist, visual and cleaning, electrical safety, function and performance, consumables, calibration, label, document","Document, label, calibrate, then inspect if time allows","Function test, release, then electrical safety at the next visit","Cleaning only, since the manufacturer handles the rest"],c:0,e:"Scheduled maintenance works the written procedure for that model: confirm the interval, visual inspection, cleaning, the electrical safety test against the limit and the last reading, function and performance checks, consumables, calibration, the label, and the record."},
{id:"u6l9-5",t:"u6l9",q:"You completed twelve scheduled inspections on Thursday but did not close the work orders. How does the department's completion rate treat them?",a:["As complete, because the work was performed","As not done, because completion is computed from closed work orders","As complete once you tell your supervisor","As complete, but only for devices that passed"],c:1,e:"Completion rate is the share of scheduled inspections closed inside their window, and it is computed from the records. Work performed and not closed counts as not done, which is why closing the work order is part of doing the work."},

// u6l10
{id:"u6l10-1",t:"u6l10",q:"A nurse calls because a warming unit on her floor is dead. Which check comes first, and why is it first?",a:["Open the case and measure the power supply output, since that is the most likely fault","Replace the fuse, since a dead device always means a blown fuse","Power at both ends, the switch, and the battery, because that is the most common cause and costs thirty seconds","Send it to the manufacturer, since warmers are complex"],c:2,e:"The first checks are ordered by probability, not by interest. Verifying that the device is plugged in at both ends, switched on, and charged costs half a minute, while opening a case costs an hour and often ends at a flat battery."},
{id:"u6l10-2",t:"u6l10",q:"A device will not power up and nothing you try changes that. What single test tells you whether the problem is the receptacle rather than the device?",a:["Measure the device's internal fuse with the power on","Reset the device to factory defaults","Swap the device's power cord with another device's cord","Plug a known-good device into the same receptacle and see whether it runs"],c:3,e:"Testing the outlet with a device you trust settles ownership of the problem in thirty seconds: if the known-good device will not run either, the receptacle belongs to facilities, and you can tell the electrician exactly where to go."},
{id:"u6l10-3",t:"u6l10",q:"You replace a blown fuse in a suction pump with an identical rating and the unit runs. What is the remaining task?",a:["Find out why the fuse blew, because a fuse that opens is a symptom rather than a part","Nothing; the repair is finished","Fit the next size up so it does not blow again","Remove the fuse holder to prevent recurrence"],c:0,e:"Replace a fuse with the same current and voltage rating, then ask why it opened. A fuse that blows twice is telling you about a short, a failing motor, or a power supply fault, and up-rating a fuse removes the protection instead of the problem."},
{id:"u6l10-4",t:"u6l10",q:"Which single test most quickly splits a monitor problem into 'device' or 'accessory'?",a:["Restarting the monitor","Swapping the suspect lead wire, probe, or cuff for a known-good one","Updating the monitor's software","Running an electrical safety test"],c:1,e:"Substitution divides the system in two, and accessories fail far more often than devices do. If a known-good accessory fixes the symptom the device is fine, and if it does not you have eliminated the most likely cause in one step."},
{id:"u6l10-5",t:"u6l10",q:"A pump's screen shows an error code you have never seen. What do you do before power cycling the unit?",a:["Reset the device to factory defaults","Clear it immediately so the floor can keep working","Record the exact wording or number and look it up in the service manual","Replace the display board"],c:2,e:"The code is the manufacturer's own troubleshooting map, and a restart erases it as often as it fixes the fault. Write down the exact wording, what the device was doing when it appeared, and look it up before changing the state of the device."},

// u7l1
{id:"u7l1-1",t:"u7l1",q:"A workstation on a nursing unit loses everything a clinician had typed whenever the power blips, though its saved files survive. Which part explains that?",a:["The motherboard, because it carries the buses","The hard drive, because platters stop spinning","The GPU, because the display is redrawn","RAM, because working memory is volatile and its contents disappear when power drops"],c:3,e:"Working memory is volatile: everything in it is gone the instant power is removed. Storage, whether a hard drive or a solid-state drive, keeps its contents with the power off, which is why saved files survive and unsaved work does not."},
{id:"u7l1-2",t:"u7l1",q:"A rolling cart computer has started clicking and occasionally cannot find its files. Which part is failing, and what would have been the better choice for a cart?",a:["The hard drive, which has spinning platters and moving heads; a solid-state drive survives bumps","The power supply; a linear supply would be better","The RAM, which rattles when loose; more memory would help","The BIOS battery; a larger coin cell would help"],c:0,e:"A hard drive stores data magnetically on spinning platters with heads flying above them, so it has moving parts, it warns by clicking or grinding, and it dislikes being bumped. A solid-state drive has no moving parts and belongs in anything mobile."},
{id:"u7l1-3",t:"u7l1",q:"After a routine update, a workstation can no longer print to the department's label printer although everything else works. What is the first suspect?",a:["The print queue is empty","The driver for that printer, since missing or wrong drivers are the classic casualty of an update","The printer's power supply","The network cable, since updates change cabling"],c:1,e:"Drivers are the small programs that let the operating system talk to one specific piece of hardware. An update that replaces or invalidates a driver breaks exactly one function, which is why 'it worked until the update' is almost always a driver story."},
{id:"u7l1-4",t:"u7l1",q:"Staff report that a shared network printer has stopped working, but its display shows ready and no errors. Where do you look first?",a:["The printer's USB cable, because network printers also use USB","The toner, because a ready display can hide an empty cartridge","The print queue, because a stuck job blocks every job behind it","The BIOS settings on each workstation"],c:2,e:"Jobs go into a queue before they reach the printer, and one stuck job stops the rest. A network printer has its own IP address and prints for everybody, so a stuck queue affects a whole floor and looks like a dead printer."},
{id:"u7l1-5",t:"u7l1",q:"A vendor engineer suggests you install a general antivirus product on an infusion pump's embedded computer to satisfy a security request. What is the correct response?",a:["Install it, since more protection is always safer","Install it on one pump as a trial and compare","Install it but disable the scanning schedule","Only the manufacturer's validated software goes on a medical device, with department approval"],c:3,e:"The computer inside a medical device is part of a regulated product validated as a whole. Unapproved software can take the device out of specification or out of service and can affect its clearance for clinical use, so validated releases and department approval govern."},

// u7l2
{id:"u7l2-1",t:"u7l2",q:"You find an unlabeled cable with a nine-pin two-row connector in a metal shell with thumbscrews. What is it, and what is it used for on medical devices?",a:["An RS-232 serial connector on a DB9 shell, used for service menus, logs, and data output","A VGA video connector, used for displays","An RJ45 network connector, used for the device segment","A DVI connector, used for imaging displays"],c:0,e:"Nine pins in two rows inside a shell with thumbscrews is a DB9, and on medical equipment it almost always carries RS-232 serial. It is how you reach a service menu, pull a log, or feed a strip printer, and a serial-to-USB adapter needs a driver."},
{id:"u7l2-2",t:"u7l2",q:"A display on an older cart shows a soft, ghosted picture that gets worse when the cable is moved. Which connector type does that symptom point at?",a:["HDMI, because digital links degrade gradually","VGA, because an analog signal degrades over a poor or long cable rather than failing cleanly","DisplayPort, because the latch has failed","RJ45, because network video is analog"],c:1,e:"VGA is analog on a blue fifteen-pin shell with thumbscrews, and analog degrades: a poor or long cable gives a soft or shadowed picture. A digital link such as HDMI or DisplayPort generally either works or shows nothing at all."},
{id:"u7l2-3",t:"u7l2",q:"Why is the link between a hospital and a detached clinic building usually run on fiber rather than copper?",a:["Fiber is cheaper per foot than twisted pair","Fiber can be spliced by any technician without tools","Fiber carries long distances at high bandwidth and gives complete electrical isolation between buildings","Copper is not allowed in healthcare facilities"],c:2,e:"Fiber carries light down glass strands, so it crosses long distances with very high bandwidth and carries no current at all. That isolation matters between buildings, where copper could carry a ground potential difference or a lightning path."},
{id:"u7l2-4",t:"u7l2",q:"A low-voltage supply brick with the right size barrel plug is available for a device whose own supply is missing. What has to match before you plug it in?",a:["Only the plug diameter, since the device regulates its own input","Only the manufacturer's logo","Only the cord length and the color","The diameter (outside and inside), the voltage, and the polarity, because a fitting plug with the wrong voltage destroys the device"],c:3,e:"Barrel plugs vary in outside diameter, inside diameter, voltage, and polarity. A plug that fits mechanically and carries the wrong voltage or reversed polarity destroys the device it fits, so all of those have to be confirmed before it goes in."},
{id:"u7l2-5",t:"u7l2",q:"A floor has used a three-prong to two-prong adapter so a monitor fits an old receptacle. What is wrong with that arrangement?",a:["It defeats the protective ground, which is never acceptable on patient-care equipment","Nothing, as long as the device works","It only matters for devices with patient leads","It is acceptable if the device is double insulated"],c:0,e:"The third prong is the protective ground, the conductor the whole electrical safety test exists to verify, and the one that carries leakage current safely away. An adapter that removes it is never acceptable on patient-care equipment."},

// u7l3
{id:"u7l3-1",t:"u7l3",q:"A central station at 10.20.4.57 with a mask of 255.255.255.0 cannot reach a server at 10.20.9.20. Are the two on the same subnet?",a:["Yes, because both addresses begin with 10.20","No, because with that mask only 10.20.4.x is local, so traffic to the server goes through the gateway","Yes, because they are in the same building","It cannot be determined from the addresses"],c:1,e:"A mask of 255.255.255.0 makes the first three numbers the network. 10.20.4 and 10.20.9 differ, so the server is on another subnet, probably another VLAN, and every packet to it crosses the default gateway."},
{id:"u7l3-2",t:"u7l3",q:"Which command do you run first on a workstation that cannot reach a server, and what does it tell you?",a:["A restart, which clears the network stack","ping, which gives the server's own address","ipconfig, which gives the machine's own address, subnet mask, and default gateway","The service manual, which lists the network settings"],c:2,e:"Everything else depends on those three numbers, so ipconfig is always the first command. Only then does a ping mean anything, because you now know which address is the gateway and whether the destination is local."},
{id:"u7l3-3",t:"u7l3",q:"You get four replies with no loss from the default gateway and 25% loss pinging a telemetry server. What does that evidence support?",a:["Replace the workstation's network cable","Reboot the workstation and try again","Change the workstation's subnet mask to reach the server","Report it to IT with the output, since everything local works and the fault is beyond the gateway"],c:3,e:"Clean replies from the gateway prove the station, its cable, the wall jack, and the local switch path are working, so the loss appears only on the path beyond the router. Handing IT the output with the jack number is faster than swapping proven-good parts."},
{id:"u7l3-4",t:"u7l3",q:"A mobile ultrasound works everywhere in the hospital except one back hallway, where it loses its wireless connection. What does that pattern indicate?",a:["A wireless coverage hole in that area rather than a device fault","A failed wireless adapter in the ultrasound","A DNS problem on the hospital network","An expired address reservation"],c:0,e:"A problem that follows a location is a coverage problem; a problem that follows the device is a device problem. The 2.4 GHz band reaches farther than 5 GHz, and coverage holes show up exactly as dropouts in one area."},
{id:"u7l3-5",t:"u7l3",q:"A nurse moved a monitor to a different wall jack in the same room and now the central station cannot see it. What is the likely explanation?",a:["The monitor needs a new power cord","The jack may land on a different VLAN than the medical device segment, so the monitor is healthy but on the wrong network","Monitors must always be plugged into the first jack in a room","The central station needs a reboot"],c:1,e:"Medical devices normally live on their own VLAN behind a firewall, and that segment is assigned per switch port. A device moved to a convenient jack can land on the wrong segment and look broken while being perfectly healthy, so device network changes go through IT."},

// u7l4
{id:"u7l4-1",t:"u7l4",q:"You want a photograph of a cracked housing to attach to a work order, and the device sits beside an occupied bed. What has to be true before you take it?",a:["Nothing; equipment photographs are always acceptable","Only that the patient has verbally agreed","No patient and no screen or paper showing patient information anywhere in the frame, including reflections","Only that the photograph is deleted afterward"],c:2,e:"Photographs of equipment are useful documentation, but a patient in the frame or a screen with patient information in the background turns it into a privacy incident. Check the whole frame, reflections included, and move the device or turn the screen if needed."},
{id:"u7l4-2",t:"u7l4",q:"Why does scanning an asset tag with a phone beat typing the asset number into the CMMS?",a:["It looks more professional to the clinical staff","It uploads the service manual to the device","It automatically closes the work order","It removes mistyped numbers and confirms which of several identical devices you are holding"],c:3,e:"Barcode and QR scanning of the asset tag opens the right record with no transcription error, which matters most on a floor with four identical pumps. The same scan logs parts and serial numbers accurately."},
{id:"u7l4-3",t:"u7l4",q:"You are heading to a basement equipment room with no signal to work on a device you have never serviced. What should you do before you go?",a:["Save the manual as an offline PDF on your phone or tablet","Memorize the troubleshooting section","Ask the vendor to call you in the room","Nothing; manuals are always available online"],c:0,e:"Service manuals and instructions for use travel as PDF files, and the rooms with the equipment problems are the rooms with no coverage. Keeping the manuals you use stored offline removes a walk back to the shop."},
{id:"u7l4-4",t:"u7l4",q:"A vendor asks you to email a device log containing patient infusion records to their personal address so they can look at it tonight. What is wrong?",a:["Nothing, as long as you delete your copy","Hospital data with patient information belongs in hospital systems, not personal accounts, and the vendor needs a proper agreement","The file is large and may bounce","Only that you should send it as a photograph instead"],c:1,e:"Moving records with patient information into personal mail or cloud storage creates an uncontrolled copy, which is exactly what the security safeguards exist to prevent. An outside company handling such data works under a business associate agreement and approved channels."},
{id:"u7l4-5",t:"u7l4",q:"Which habit with the CMMS mobile app most improves a department's records?",a:["Taking a photograph of every device you pass","Opening every work order at the start of the shift","Closing the work order where you did the work, with the readings and the exact error wording","Writing all notes up on Friday afternoon"],c:2,e:"Notes written at the bedside contain the code as it appeared, the readings as taken, and what the user actually said. The same note reconstructed from memory hours later loses exactly those details, and within a week it loses all of them."},

// u7l5
{id:"u7l5-1",t:"u7l5",q:"While servicing a monitor you recognize the patient's name as a neighbor and open the chart to see how they are doing. What have you done?",a:["Nothing wrong, since you have system access","Something acceptable because you did not share it","Something acceptable if you tell the nurse afterward","A privacy violation, because access is limited to what your job requires and it is audited"],c:3,e:"Minimum necessary means you use only what the job requires: the device, its settings, its logs, and its asset number. Curiosity with no bad intent is still a violation, and access to records is logged and reviewed."},
{id:"u7l5-2",t:"u7l5",q:"An integrated monitor is reassigned to a new bed but keeps the previous patient's assignment in the system. What is the consequence?",a:["Vital signs will be charted into the wrong patient's record, where a clinician may act on them","The chart will simply be blank until it is corrected","Only the monitor's trend display is affected","The interface will reject the data automatically"],c:0,e:"Device integration carries readings through an interface or middleware server into the electronic chart, attached to a patient and an encounter. A wrong assignment does not produce a blank chart, it produces a wrong one, so confirming patient and bed assignment is part of moving a device."},
{id:"u7l5-3",t:"u7l5",q:"A vendor is collecting three ultrasound units for trade-in next week. What must happen before they leave the building?",a:["Nothing, because the vendor signs for the equipment","Clear the stored patient data by the manufacturer's documented procedure, or wipe or destroy the storage, and keep the record","Remove the asset tags and close the records","Only delete the most recent studies"],c:1,e:"Ultrasound, monitors, pumps, and defibrillators all store patient data. A device leaving for trade-in, loan return, depot repair, or disposal needs its data cleared or the storage destroyed by an approved process, with the certificate or record retained."},
{id:"u7l5-4",t:"u7l5",q:"Which standard do the interfaces that carry device data into the electronic chart typically speak?",a:["RS-232","DHCP","HL7","WPA3"],c:2,e:"HL7 is the long-standing messaging standard that interface engines and middleware use to move patient and clinical data between systems, including the vital signs and infusion data your equipment produces."},
{id:"u7l5-5",t:"u7l5",q:"An outside service organization will start working on your integrated monitors next month. What has to be in place first?",a:["A purchase order only","A copy of the service manual for their files","Nothing, as long as their technicians are certified","A business associate agreement, since the company will handle protected health information"],c:3,e:"Any outside company that handles protected health information signs a business associate agreement binding it to the same protections, and it is signed before the work begins rather than afterward."},

// u7l6
{id:"u7l6-1",t:"u7l6",q:"An email that appears to come from a device manufacturer warns of an urgent recall and asks you to sign in through a link to confirm your account. What do you do?",a:["Do not click, do not reply, report it the way your facility asks, and reach the vendor on a number you already have","Reply asking the sender to confirm it is genuine","Forward it to the clinical floors so they are aware","Sign in quickly, since recalls are time sensitive"],c:0,e:"Urgency plus a link plus a credential request is the shape of phishing, and service notices are used against technicians precisely because we open them. Report it, and verify anything that looks real but unexpected through a contact you already hold."},
{id:"u7l6-2",t:"u7l6",q:"Why do hospitals place medical devices on their own VLAN behind a firewall?",a:["To make them faster than office computers","Because many of them cannot be patched or hardened, so the segment limits what can reach them and what they could reach","Because regulations forbid devices on the same wire as computers","So that vendors can connect to them directly from outside"],c:1,e:"A firewall allows only permitted traffic at a boundary, and the device segment sits behind one because many devices run software that cannot be updated. The segment contains the damage in both directions, which is also why device network changes go through IT."},
{id:"u7l6-3",t:"u7l6",q:"Your facility patches workstations monthly. Does the same schedule apply to the infusion pump fleet?",a:["Yes, every networked system follows the same schedule","Yes, but only for pumps that are on the network","No, medical devices receive only the manufacturer's validated updates on agreed terms","No, medical devices are never updated at all"],c:2,e:"Workstations and servers get patched promptly, but a medical device's clearance rests on the configuration it was validated in, so it receives only validated updates. When a device cannot be patched, the segment, monitoring, and restricted accounts are the compensating controls."},
{id:"u7l6-4",t:"u7l6",q:"You find a USB drive on a chair in a nurse station and want to see whose it is. What is the right action?",a:["Plug it into a clinical workstation to check the file names","Plug it into your own laptop, which has antivirus","Plug it into a medical device, which is isolated anyway","Hand it to the department or security per your facility's process, without plugging it in anywhere"],c:3,e:"An unknown drive is a way to carry malware past every network control by hand. No unknown or found drives in any clinical machine, and no personal drives for moving device data: use the method your department approves."},
{id:"u7l6-5",t:"u7l6",q:"A workstation in your shop displays a ransom message and files have been renamed. What should you do?",a:["Report it immediately on the published number, then follow the security team's instructions, which may include disconnecting it while leaving it powered","Power it off at once and reimage it to save time","Try to decrypt the files yourself before reporting","Disconnect it and say nothing until you understand it"],c:0,e:"Report at once with what you saw and when, then follow the security team's direction. Reimaging or powering down destroys the evidence of how the intrusion happened, and fast reporting is the difference between one machine and a hospital-wide outage."},

// u8l1
{id:"u8l1-1",t:"u8l1",q:"You want the broadest early exposure to many equipment families and do not mind driving between sites. Which employer fits best?",a:["A manufacturer's field service team on one product line","An independent service organization contracting across several facilities","An imaging service specialist group","A single surgery center with a small fleet"],c:1,e:"An independent service organization services equipment under contract for several facilities, so the range of equipment is wide and comes quickly, with more travel and more working alone than a hospital department."},
{id:"u8l1-2",t:"u8l1",q:"Which employer trains a technician from zero with no prior electronics background and then puts them on a wide equipment fleet?",a:["An imaging service group, which starts at the high-voltage bench","Manufacturer field service, which requires factory certification first","The military, through the Department of Defense biomedical equipment program","A dialysis organization, which requires a degree"],c:2,e:"The armed forces train their own biomedical equipment technicians and put them to work immediately on a broad fleet. It is the most structured route into the field, and the training transfers directly to hospital work afterward."},
{id:"u8l1-3",t:"u8l1",q:"Hospital job searches for 'biomed' return nothing in your area. What is the practical next step?",a:["Assume the market is closed and wait six months","Only check the large national job boards","Apply for nursing roles instead","Search the other titles the same job uses, such as biomedical equipment technician, equipment technician, clinical engineering technician, and field service technician"],c:3,e:"The same work is posted under half a dozen titles, and searching one of them hides most of the market. Support titles such as equipment support specialist also exist and are a genuine way in."},
{id:"u8l1-4",t:"u8l1",q:"A hiring manager interviewing entry-level candidates says device knowledge is not what she screens for. What is she screening for instead?",a:["Safety habits, documentation, and attitude","Years of unrelated work history","Typing speed and spreadsheet skills","Willingness to work unpaid overtime"],c:0,e:"A department expects to train a new technician on its own equipment and procedures. What it cannot train quickly is doing the safety test the same way every time, writing a record a stranger can follow, and asking before guessing."},
{id:"u8l1-5",t:"u8l1",q:"Besides public postings, which routes account for a large share of first biomed jobs?",a:["Walking into a hospital and asking at the front desk","Referrals, apprenticeships and trainee programs, veteran hiring programs, degree program rotations, and staffing agencies","Cold calling manufacturers' corporate offices","Waiting for a recruiter to make contact"],c:1,e:"Hiring managers are trying to reduce the risk of an unknown entry candidate, so anything that makes you known helps: a referral, a clinical rotation that becomes the interview, a trainee program, or a contract project that turns permanent."},

// u8l2
{id:"u8l2-1",t:"u8l2",q:"How long should an entry-level biomed resume be, and what should the skills section use for wording?",a:["Two pages, written in your own technical phrasing","Three pages, listing every course you have taken","One page, with a skills section in the words the job posting uses","One page, with no skills section at all"],c:2,e:"Keep it to one page, because an entry resume on two pages reads as padding. The first reader is often a keyword filter and the second a manager scanning for familiar nouns, so use the posting's own terms truthfully."},
{id:"u8l2-2",t:"u8l2",q:"What should go into the lab portfolio you bring to a biomed interview?",a:["Reference letters only","A list of the devices you hope to work on","Printouts of the manufacturer manuals you have read","Your lab records with the readings and the limits applied, kit photographs, the certificate, and the credential"],c:3,e:"Recorded measurements are what other entry candidates do not have: safety test readings in each condition with their limits, a flow check with its percent error, defibrillator energies against the analyzer, and monitor verification against simulator settings."},
{id:"u8l2-3",t:"u8l2",q:"A posting repeatedly uses the words preventive maintenance, incoming inspection, and work orders. What should you do with those terms?",a:["Make sure each one appears truthfully in your resume, and be ready to discuss every line","Avoid them, so the resume does not look copied","Use them only in the cover letter","Replace them with more advanced-sounding terms"],c:0,e:"Keyword matching gets a resume past the first filter, and the standard is honesty: every noun on the page must be something you can talk about for two minutes. Claiming a term you cannot discuss fails in the first five minutes of the interview."},
{id:"u8l2-4",t:"u8l2",q:"Your only prior job was two years in retail customer service. How should the resume treat it?",a:["Leave it off, since it is not technical","Include it, naming the transferable parts: following written procedures, careful documentation, and dealing with upset people","Include it but describe it as electronics work","Include only the dates with no description"],c:1,e:"Hospitals care about habits as much as hands. Following procedures exactly, documenting carefully, and staying calm with frustrated people are genuinely relevant to a bedside job, so name them plainly rather than hiding the history."},
{id:"u8l2-5",t:"u8l2",q:"What is the right way to handle references for a first biomed job?",a:["List anyone who knows you, without telling them","Only family members, who will always speak well of you","Three people who will answer the phone, asked in advance and told which job you applied for","No references, since employers rarely call"],c:2,e:"References are only useful if they answer and know what the role needs. Ask first, tell them the job and what it requires, and thank them whatever the outcome, because the next opening is a few months away."},

// u8l3
{id:"u8l3-1",t:"u8l3",q:"An interviewer asks you to describe a time you made a mistake at work. What structure should the answer follow?",a:["Say you cannot think of one, which shows carefulness","Describe someone else's mistake that you fixed","Give a long technical explanation of the equipment involved","STAR: the situation, the task, the action you took, and the result, including who you told and what you changed"],c:3,e:"Behavioral questions are answered in STAR order and kept under two minutes. On the mistake question the answer they want is that you report things: what happened, when you noticed, who you told immediately, and what you changed."},
{id:"u8l3-2",t:"u8l3",q:"At a hands-on station you are handed a device and an electrical safety analyzer. What is the best way to work?",a:["Narrate the order as you go: visual and cord, ground resistance, chassis leakage in each condition, then lead leakage if there are applied parts","Work silently and quickly to show confidence","Start with chassis leakage, the most important measurement","Ask the interviewer to tell you the limits first and then guess"],c:0,e:"The interviewer is watching method and safety more than the final number. Saying the order out loud shows you have one and follow it, and starting with the visual and cord check demonstrates the habit they most want to see."},
{id:"u8l3-3",t:"u8l3",q:"During a practical you are asked for the tolerance on a measurement you do not know. What is the best answer?",a:["Give your best guess confidently, since hesitating looks weak","Say that the service manual would give the tolerance and ask whether you may look it up","Say the tolerance is always plus or minus 5%","Say the question is unfair for an entry candidate"],c:1,e:"Nobody wants a technician who invents specifications. Looking up a value rather than guessing is one of the four things an interviewer is watching for, along with method, visible safety habits, and plain communication."},
{id:"u8l3-4",t:"u8l3",q:"A practical station hands you a multimeter and asks you to check a supply rail and then a fuse. How do you use it correctly?",a:["Ohms on the rail with the power on, then amps on the fuse","Amps across the rail, then volts on the fuse","Volts across the rail with the power on, then ohms or continuity on the fuse with the power off","Continuity on both, with the power on for speed"],c:2,e:"Voltage is measured across a component with the circuit energized, current is measured in series, and resistance and continuity are measured only with the power off. Getting that wrong at an interview station is read as a safety problem."},
{id:"u8l3-5",t:"u8l3",q:"The interview ends and you are invited to ask questions. Which set is appropriate?",a:["How much overtime pay is available and how soon you can take leave","Whether the department has had any lawsuits","Nothing, since questions suggest you were not listening","How the maintenance workload is distributed, what training looks like in six months, which equipment families you would start on, and how escalation to a vendor works"],c:3,e:"Questions about the work show you are thinking about the job rather than only the offer. Pay and time off belong to the offer conversation, and a short thank-you naming something specific from the interview should follow within a day."},

// u8l4
{id:"u8l4-1",t:"u8l4",q:"What should a new technician learn in the very first week on the job?",a:["The CMMS: opening, updating, and closing a work order, finding device history, and the scheduled backlog","Every error code for the monitor fleet","The manufacturer's full schematic set","The hospital's budget process"],c:0,e:"The record is the work, so the system that holds it comes first. Learning to close a work order cleanly and keeping your scheduled list current earns trust faster than a brilliant repair with a blank record."},
{id:"u8l4-2",t:"u8l4",q:"A call turns out to be a setup step the nurse had missed, and the device is fine. What goes in the record?",a:["Nothing, since no repair was done","A note that there was no fault, what you showed the user, and that the device was returned to service","A note blaming the user so the pattern is clear","Only the time spent, with no description"],c:1,e:"Documenting the nothing calls is how a training need becomes visible: three similar notes from one unit is a problem somebody can fix. Write what it was without blame, because that nurse deciding to call you next time matters more than being right."},
{id:"u8l4-3",t:"u8l4",q:"Which of these is a record, and which is a note that will be useless in a year?",a:["'Safety test passed' is a record; the readings are optional detail","Both are equally useful records","'Ground 0.09 ohm, chassis 30/44/120/150 microamps, limit 500, NFPA 99' is the record; 'safety test passed' is not","Neither belongs in the work order"],c:2,e:"Readings with their units, conditions, and the limit they were judged against let the next technician see a trend and let a surveyor see a real test. The word passed hides everything, including a device that is drifting toward failure."},
{id:"u8l4-4",t:"u8l4",q:"You realize you returned a device to a floor before completing its safety test. What should you do?",a:["Wait to see whether anyone notices","Quietly retrieve it and test it without telling anybody","Note it in the work order next week","Report it to your supervisor immediately, in plain words, with the device, its location, and what you have done to make it safe"],c:3,e:"Hospitals have processes for exactly this, and they protect patients and you. What ends careers is not the mistake, it is the hidden mistake that somebody else discovers later."},
{id:"u8l4-5",t:"u8l4",q:"Your department keeps a training log and a list of signed competencies. What do they govern?",a:["Which devices and procedures you are cleared to work on alone, and you work inside that list","Only your pay grade","Which floors you may walk on","Only the annual review paperwork"],c:0,e:"Competency lists exist so nobody works beyond what they have been signed off for. Ask what is on yours, ask what it takes to add to it, and expect a probation review around ninety days measuring completion, documentation, safety habits, and how the floors talk about you."},

// u8l5
{id:"u8l5-1",t:"u8l5",q:"How long is the associate credential valid, and what does that validity period imply for your planning?",a:["3 years, renewable with continuing education","5 years, non-renewable, so plan the technician exam inside that window","10 years, renewable by a short refresher","It never expires, so no planning is needed"],c:1,e:"The associate credential is valid five years and does not renew, which is deliberate: five years of work is more than the four that qualifies you for full technician certification on experience alone, so the clock is a schedule rather than a penalty."},
{id:"u8l5-2",t:"u8l5",q:"You were hired with no degree and have worked two years full time as a biomed. Where do you stand on the technician credential?",a:["Full certification is already available with two years of work","Nothing is available without an associate degree","Candidate status is available now, with five years to upgrade; full certification comes at four years of work alone","Only the imaging credential is available"],c:2,e:"Candidate status opens at a qualifying associate degree alone, a United States military biomedical equipment technology program alone, or two years of full-time work alone, with five years to finish. Full certification needs the degree or program plus two years, or four years of work alone."},
{id:"u8l5-3",t:"u8l5",q:"What is the format and schedule of the technician-level exam above the associate credential?",a:["100 questions in 2 hours, remote proctored year-round","50 questions in 1 hour, offered on demand","200 questions in 4 hours, offered quarterly","165 questions in 3 hours, offered in May and November at test centers"],c:3,e:"The technician exam is 165 questions in three hours at a test center, offered in May and November, $410 for members and $460 for non-members, and recertified every three years on continuing education. A missed window costs six months."},
{id:"u8l5-4",t:"u8l5",q:"A colleague wants to move into radiography and fluoroscopy service. Which credential matches that path, and which one is for running a department?",a:["CRES for imaging; CHTM for managing a program","CHTM for imaging; CRES for managing a program","Both are imaging credentials","Neither exists; the technician credential covers both"],c:0,e:"CRES is the imaging specialist branch, taken after imaging service experience, and is also 165 questions in three hours. CHTM is the management branch, covering budgets, staffing, contracts, regulation, and program strategy."},
{id:"u8l5-5",t:"u8l5",q:"Why does AAMI membership tend to pay for itself for someone climbing this ladder?",a:["It guarantees a job placement","Members pay a lower fee on every ACI exam, and membership brings the publications, webinars, and job listings that keep you current","It removes the experience requirements","It provides free service manuals for every device"],c:1,e:"The member and non-member fees differ at every level, so membership generally pays for itself the first time you sit an exam, and continuing education is both a recertification requirement above the associate level and the actual mechanism of getting better."}
);
